Dataset testing evaluated and tested the algorithms
based on the condition of how they can learn patterns
from noisy and messy real world data. It had shown that
KNN and Bernoulli were able to learn the patterns well.

Manual testing pointed out the biggest issue prevalent
in real world symptoms reporting: symptoms ambiguousness.
And it had shown that LR, Multinomial, Gaussian and Light
could handle such ambiguousness better.

Now, it's whose word over whose. It is manual testing's
word over dataset testing's word because the former
actually evaluates the algorithms on the issue
that is more relevant in my case.

For deployment, it doesn't matter how well the algorithms
can learn the patterns. The only thing that matters is
how will they handle the symptoms ambiguousness;
which is the most relevant issue.

So, in favour of manual testing, the LR, Multinomial,
Gaussian and LightGBM will be ensembled.



A proper evaluation of manual testing results and the dataset testing results(at a single corrupted rate vs multiple corrupted rate)
are to be done. This will give full confidence in which estimators to select to form an ensemble for deployment.
